# Why I Am Building Boogle

## My Motivation

Boogle is a personal project that I started to improve my confidence in programming and software development.

In the age of AI, I sometimes found myself confused about how I should approach coding. There were times when I relied too heavily on AI assistance, and other times when I avoided using it altogether because I felt that relying on it would prevent me from learning. This often left me struggling, feeling behind, and uncertain about my own programming abilities.

I wanted to find a better balance.

## Learning With AI, Not Depending on It

With Boogle, my goal is to learn how to use AI as a development assistant while still understanding the code I write.

I want to become comfortable with asking for guidance, exploring unfamiliar concepts, reviewing suggestions, debugging problems, and making my own technical decisions.

AI should help me learn and become more productive, not replace the learning process itself.

I believe that becoming a good developer is not about writing every line without assistance. It is about understanding what you build, knowing why you build it, and being able to solve problems independently when necessary.

## A Project Built for Learning

Boogle is not intended to be something revolutionary or commercially ambitious. I am building it primarily for fun, experimentation, and personal growth.

Through this project, I want to:

- Improve my programming fundamentals.
- Gain practical experience in software development.
- Understand application architecture and how different technologies work together.
- Become better at debugging, problem-solving, and making technical decisions.
- Develop a healthy and productive relationship with AI-assisted coding.
- Build confidence by turning my ideas into working software.

## My Goal

My goal is simple: **learn by building, improve through mistakes, and become a more confident developer.**

Boogle is part of that journey. It does not need to be perfect, and I do not need to know everything before starting. What matters is continuing to learn, experiment, and make progress.

This project is for the experience, the learning, and the fun of building something of my own.